1
00:00:01,639 --> 00:00:05,200
Dr. Dorian.

2
00:00:07,318 --> 00:00:12,520
The next day was Saturday. Fern stood at the kitchen sink drying the breakfast

3
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:15,239
dishes as her mother washed them. Mrs.

4
00:00:15,239 --> 00:00:17,879
Arable worked silently.

5
00:00:17,879 --> 00:00:22,760
She hoped Fern would go out and play with other children instead of heading

6
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:28,398
for the Zuckerman's barn to sit and watch animals.

7
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:35,079
Charlotte is the best storyteller I ever heard," said Fern, poking her dish towel

8
00:00:35,079 --> 00:00:41,318
into a cereal bowl. "Fern," said her mother sternly, "you must not invent

9
00:00:41,318 --> 00:00:49,079
things. You know, spiders don't tell stories. Spiders can't talk. Charlotte

10
00:00:49,079 --> 00:00:57,639
can, replied Fern. She doesn't talk very loud, but she talks. What kind of story

11
00:00:57,639 --> 00:01:02,520
did she tell? asked Mrs. Arable.

12
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:09,879
Well, began Fern, she told us about a cousin of hers who caught a fish in her

13
00:01:09,879 --> 00:01:17,799
web. Don't you think that's fascinating, Fern? Dear, how would a fish get into a

14
00:01:17,799 --> 00:01:24,920
spider's web? said Mrs. Arable. You know it couldn't happen. You're making this

15
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:33,639
up. Oh, it happened. All right, replied Fern. Charlotte never fibs. This cousin

16
00:01:33,639 --> 00:01:39,239
of hers built a web across a stream.

17
00:01:39,239 --> 00:01:45,719
One day she was hanging around on the web and a tiny fish leaped into the air

18
00:01:45,719 --> 00:01:53,398
and got tangled in the web. The fish was caught by one fin mother. Its tail was

19
00:01:53,398 --> 00:01:57,159
wildly thrashing and shining in the sun.

20
00:01:57,159 --> 00:02:05,000
Can't you just see the web sagging dangerously under the weight of the fish?

21
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,439
Charlotte's cousin kept slipping in, dodging out, and she was beaten

22
00:02:10,439 --> 00:02:17,559
mercilessly over the head by the wildly thrashing fish. Dancing in, dancing out,

23
00:02:17,559 --> 00:02:27,719
throwing Fern snapped her mother. Stop it. Stop these wild tails. I'm not inventing,

24
00:02:27,719 --> 00:02:32,439
said Fern. I'm just telling you the facts.

25
00:02:32,439 --> 00:02:41,000
What finally happened?" asked her mother, whose curiosity began to get the better of her.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,478
Charlotte's cousin won. She wrapped the fish up, then she ate him when she got

27
00:02:47,478 --> 00:02:54,680
good and ready. Spiders have to eat the same as the rest of us. "Yes, I suppose

28
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:59,398
they do," said Mrs. Arable vaguely.

29
00:02:59,398 --> 00:03:04,040
Charlotte has another cousin who is a balloonist.

30
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:10,680
She stands on her head, lets out a lot of line, and is carried aloft on the

31
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:17,398
wind. Mother, wouldn't you simply love to do that?

32
00:03:17,398 --> 00:03:25,079
Yes, I would, come to think of it, replied Mrs. Arable. But Fern, darling,

33
00:03:25,079 --> 00:03:30,840
I wish you would play outdoors today instead of going to Uncle Homer's barn.

34
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:35,959
Find some of your playmates and do something nice outdoors. You're spending

35
00:03:35,959 --> 00:03:43,159
too much time in that barn. It isn't good for you to be alone so much. Alone,

36
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:51,000
said Fern. Alone? My best friends are in the barn cellar. It is a very sociable

37
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,559
place. Not at all lonely.

38
00:03:55,559 --> 00:04:00,840
Fern disappeared after a while, walking down the road towards Zuckerman's. Her

39
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:06,280
mother dusted the sitting room. As she worked, she kept thinking about Fern. It

40
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:12,439
didn't seem natural for a little girl to be so interested in animals. Finally,

41
00:04:12,439 --> 00:04:18,600
Mrs. Arable made up her mind. She would pay a call on old Dr. Dorian and ask his

42
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:20,040
advice.

43
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:25,478
She got in the car and drove to his office in the village.

44
00:04:25,478 --> 00:04:30,840
Dr. Dorian had a thick beard. He was glad to see Mrs. Arable and gave her a

45
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:36,760
comfortable chair. "It's about Fern," she explained.

46
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:46,359
"Fern spends entirely too much time in the Zuckermanman's barn. It doesn't seem normal.

47
00:04:46,359 --> 00:04:51,719
She sits on a milk stool in a corner of the barn cellar near the pig pin and

48
00:04:51,719 --> 00:04:58,680
watches animals hour after hour. She just sits and listens. Dr. Dorian leaned

49
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:02,600
back and closed his eyes.

50
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:11,879
"How enchanting," he said. "It must be real nice and quiet down there.

51
00:05:11,879 --> 00:05:19,000
Homer has some sheep, hasn't he?" Yes, said Mrs. Arable. But it all started

52
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,359
with that pig we let Fern raise on a bottle. She calls him Wilbur.

53
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:31,719
Homer bought the pig, and ever since it left our place, Fern has been going down

54
00:05:31,719 --> 00:05:39,719
to her uncles to be near it. I've been hearing things about that pig, said Dr.

55
00:05:39,719 --> 00:05:47,719
Dorian, opening his eyes. They say he's quite a pig. "Have you heard about the

56
00:05:47,719 --> 00:05:53,079
words that appeared in the spider's web?" asked Mrs. Arable nervously.

57
00:05:53,079 --> 00:06:02,439
"Yes," replied the doctor. "Well, do you understand it?" asked Mrs. Arable.

58
00:06:02,439 --> 00:06:08,840
"Understand what? Do you understand how there could be any writen in a spider's

59
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:18,120
web?" Oh no, said Dr. Dorian. I don't understand it. But for that matter, I

60
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:24,280
don't understand how a spider learned to spin a web in the first place.

61
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:32,920
When the words appeared, everyone said they were a miracle, but nobody pointed out that the web

62
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:36,760
itself is a miracle.

63
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:40,280
What's miraculous about a spider's web?

64
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:47,879
said Mrs. Arable. I don't see why you say a web is a miracle. It's just a web.

65
00:06:47,879 --> 00:06:51,159
Ever tried to spin one? asked Dr.

66
00:06:51,159 --> 00:07:00,359
Dorian. Mrs. Arable shifted uneasily in her chair. No, she replied. But I can

67
00:07:00,359 --> 00:07:04,359
crochet a doily and I can knit a sock.

68
00:07:04,359 --> 00:07:13,799
Sure, said the doctor. But somebody taught you, didn't they? My mother taught me.

69
00:07:13,799 --> 00:07:17,239
Well, who taught a spider?

70
00:07:17,239 --> 00:07:24,280
A young spider knows how to spin a web without any instruction from anybody.

71
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:32,600
Don't you regard that as a miracle? I suppose so, said Mrs. Arable. I never

72
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:38,120
looked at it that way before. Still, I don't understand how those words got

73
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:46,000
into the web. I don't understand it, and I don't like what I can't understand.

74
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:50,840
None of us do, said Dr. Dorian, sighing.

75
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:58,439
I'm a doctor. Doctors are supposed to understand everything. But I don't

76
00:07:58,439 --> 00:08:07,318
understand everything and I don't intend to let it worry me. Mrs. Arable fidgeted.

77
00:08:07,318 --> 00:08:12,520
Fern says the animals talk to each other, Dr. Dorian.

78
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:17,239
Do you believe animals talk?

79
00:08:17,239 --> 00:08:24,040
I never heard one say anything, he replied. But that proves nothing. It is

80
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:29,398
quite possible that an animal has spoken civily to me and that I didn't catch the

81
00:08:29,398 --> 00:08:33,159
remark because I wasn't paying attention.

82
00:08:33,159 --> 00:08:36,840
Children pay better attention than grown-ups.

83
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:42,040
If Fern says that the animals in Zuckerman's barn talk, I'm quite ready

84
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:49,559
to believe her. Perhaps if people talked less, animals would talk more.

85
00:08:49,559 --> 00:08:56,680
People are incessant talkers. I can give you my word on that.

86
00:08:56,680 --> 00:09:02,680
Well, I feel better about Fern, said Mrs. Arable. You don't think I need to

87
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,200
worry about her? Does she look well?

88
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,559
asked the doctor? Oh, yes.

89
00:09:09,559 --> 00:09:17,478
Appetite good? Oh, yes, she's always hungry. Sleep well at night? Oh, yes.

90
00:09:17,478 --> 00:09:23,639
Then don't worry, said the doctor. Do you think she'll ever start thinking

91
00:09:23,639 --> 00:09:31,318
about something besides pigs and sheep and geese and spiders?

92
00:09:31,318 --> 00:09:33,639
How old is Fern?

93
00:09:33,639 --> 00:09:35,478
She's eight.

94
00:09:35,478 --> 00:09:43,879
Well, said Dr. Dorian, I think she will always love animals, but I doubt that

95
00:09:43,879 --> 00:09:49,559
she spends her entire life in Homer Zuckerman's barn cellar.

96
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:53,478
How about boys? Does she know any boys?

97
00:09:53,478 --> 00:09:59,239
She knows Henry Fussy, said Mrs. Arable brightly.

98
00:09:59,239 --> 00:10:05,318
Dr. Dorian closed his eyes again and went into deep thought.

99
00:10:05,318 --> 00:10:12,040
Henry Fussy, he mumbled. H remarkable.

100
00:10:12,040 --> 00:10:19,239
Well, I don't think you have anything to worry about. Let Fern associate with her

101
00:10:19,239 --> 00:10:26,040
friends in the barn if she wants to. I would say offh hand that spiders and

102
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:32,760
pigs were fully as interesting as Henry Fussy. Yet I predict that the day will

103
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:40,680
come when even Henry will drop some chance remark that catches Fern's attention.

104
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:45,318
It's amazing how children change from year to year.

105
00:10:45,318 --> 00:10:53,079
How's Avery? he asked, opening his eyes wide. "Oh, Avery," chuckled Mrs. Arable.

106
00:10:53,079 --> 00:10:59,079
"Avery is always fine. Of course, he gets into poison ivy and gets stung by

107
00:10:59,079 --> 00:11:04,840
wasps and bees and brings frogs and snakes home and breaks everything he

108
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:11,879
lays his hands on. "He's fine." "Good," said the doctor. Mrs. Arable said

109
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:20,799
goodbye and thanked Dr. Dorian very much for his advice. She felt greatly relieved.

110
00:11:21,079 --> 00:11:22,000
Chapter 15.
